{
  "source": "Ours (GROUND.md §6.6). The Tin Finch TF-01's sizes, sockets, skids and nozzles come from its asset (D:/GameDev/Assets/TinFinch_TF01: socket_manifest.json and the source geometry, in the game model's axes: +Z the nose, +Y up, +X the left side, metres from the pivot on the ground between the skids). Mass, thrust, rates and drag are chosen for a weighty, thrust-borne skiff flown the Elite way (flight assist on by default, boost); drag areas are Cd x area. The hull's 750 is Rust's minicopter's; it burns lamp oil, our low-grade fuel, as Rust's helicopters burn theirs. The air comes from the planet (the zone's spec), not from here. P2 (GROUND_SYSTEMS.md §6): placed from its item (placement.json's ship rule with its own 'placement' on top), its hull must be clear and its skids on the ground or a foundation's top no steeper than 20 degrees (the vehicle workbench bay's slope), within 12 m of the eye; it carries fuel.placed of lamp oil, the same 50 its recipe's first tank holds. The Iron Wake (IRON_WAKE.md §5.4, IW1): a carrier people walk in. Its numbers are §5.4's proposals; its model, ten sweep boxes, four landing pads, sockets and interior (convex pieces by part, boarding volume, rooms) come from its data file, ships/iron-wake.json, written by its import; its centre of mass is the asset's hint (the data file's, checked); it floats on 1,680 m3 in a band 4.1 m below and 2.3 m above its centre of mass, and flight assist holds it within 20 degrees of pitch and 12 of roll. Seats, doors and parts (IRON_WAKE.md §5.1, IW2): a point is a socket of the data file by name or three numbers; a seat's role flies (helm), rides (passenger) or works a station of the data file (station:<id>); its verb is the interaction verb that sits there, its sitters who may (anyone; unlocked: anyone while the ship is unlocked, its crew while locked; crew: those on its list, authorised at its helm); a door drives parts of the data file by its progress (or the views sample the model's clip), opens always, still or slow (at rest, or under 5 m/s within 30 m of the ground) and shuts any time; parts are the targets a TargetRef's part names, in their wire order, the first the hull, each met by a ball (at, radius) or by pieces of the data file. Health: the ramp and the airlock as the building's armoured door (1,000, protection armoured, a blast's doors' table, mended with refined metal, 10 from nothing); a battery 1,500 of metal, mended with metal fragments. Plume sizes are what ShipView drew, the Tin Finch's scaled by the square root of a ship's size over the Tin Finch's. Lamps: the Tin Finch's nav lights, beacon and landing light where ShipView put them; the Iron Wake's data file's fixtures, warm white (the lamp material's colour), the four on Deck A and the bridge's two casting shadows, dim red emergency lamps without power (IRON_WAKE.md §9.4); drawn from IW6. Repair (VEHICLES.md §5.5): the hammer mends a hull as it mends a building's pieces, 100 a hit after 30 s without damage, for 0.2 metal fragments a point of health (the Tin Finch 150 from nothing, the Iron Wake 3,600). Sockets (VEHICLES.md §9.1): where modules go, none yet.",
